Omarchy's "Time to recharge!" warning is critical, so it never expires, and
the notification service restores it after every shell restart: a warning
from a battery that was low once kept showing while the Mac charged. The
battery service now dismisses it whenever the battery is not low (charging,
on AC, above the threshold or gone) and a warning may still be showing,
which is also the case at every shell start, for a restored warning. It
decides only once UPower has read the display device (`ready`), from the
device's own state: right after a start the device holds defaults that look
like a battery that is gone or charging, and dismissing then closed a
restored warning while the battery was still low. Readiness and state
changes trigger a check, so charging closes the warning at once. It
dismisses through `omarchy-shell notifications dismiss` without -q, and
forgets the warning only when that succeeds, so a dismissal the starting
shell did not take is sent again on the next battery check. The warning and
its dismissal run one at a time, in order. The notification service applies
a dismissal that arrives before its restore has run to the popups it
restores, so a dismissal at shell start does not race the restore.
Omarchy's battery and notifications tests cover it.

Remove once the pinned Omarchy includes it (offered to omacom/omarchy).
